On Sun, Feb 20, 2011 at 11:31:22AM -0500, Luke Bergen wrote:
>    In all honesty I hope to see more lujvo like this. It's about time that
>    lojban started forging ahead with original words and ideas and stop
>    worrying solely about having a word for every nuance of every english idea
>    that we can come up with. And don't take this one personally .arpis., I've
>    been harping on this one for a while.

I appreciated the idea of creating original words in the context
of the conversation about the word "cfisisku" (on the jbovlaste
mailing list), I think this word, "nikyge'u", is a poor example
of this practice.

There isn't anything essential to the idea of a story containing
exaggeration in that word.  It is no different than "nickle cat"
or "gold dog." What metric do I use to determine whether this word
is a better definition than any alternate choice? The definition
is arbitrary.

This word is random; another randomly selected lujvo could be
equally plausible.  I think that new words should have a minimum
level of plausibility such that a comparison to other choices can
be made.

-Alan
